# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository had no .gitattributes and `core.autocrlf` is false, so git
# stored whatever bytes a tool wrote. Commits from Windows editors left CRLF in
# a handful of blobs (see `git log -- crates/alnair-router/src/upstream/probe.rs`),
# which made one-line edits show up as whole-file rewrites.
#
# Everything textual is stored as LF. Checkout also uses LF on every platform:
# the Rust and Node toolchains both accept it, and a uniform repository avoids
# the mixed-ending churn entirely.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never diff, never convert.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.webp  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.bmp   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.tar   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary

# Windows batch scripts must keep CRLF: cmd.exe reads a bare LF as part of the
# line. Powershell is not affected — `install.ps1` is fetched with `irm | iex`
# and never touches disk — so it stays LF like any other text file.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
